From 321a5cc47353dfe3bfb135015497d33421aa3ce4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nassos Yiannopoulos <nassosyian@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:18:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 75/75] hw/display/pvg: guest resource flags, shared
 IOSurfaces, async completion

Upload skipping driven by the guest (resource-flags, default on): Apple's
host keeps every resource on the GPU and re-uploads only what the guest
reports as CPU-modified (ExecIndirect2 resource entries bit 0,
InvalidateResources 0x34, which was ignored). An image equal to the
guest's copy is reused until then; executor writes and mapping changes
invalidate it by range and, through any mapping, by guest RAM page
(per-page write sequence). 59% of uploaded bytes were repeats.

share-surfaces (default on): one host image per IOSurface backing,
plane and view shape for the texture objects of every task.

async-cmds (default on): task-state commands (Map/Unmap, Delete*,
DefineTask, SetObjectList, Invalidate/SynchronizeResources,
ReplacePhysical) and the command-buffer reads of ExecIndirect2 run on
the worker in FIFO order instead of draining it on the vCPU with the BQL
held (~100 drains, 70-80 ms per 500 command buffers; doorbells now 2 us).

async-visible (default on): command buffers whose guest-visible writes
are GPU scatters signal a timeline semaphore and return; a completion
thread publishes their stamps in order once reached. CPU readers wait
per guest page. irqfd (default on): completion interrupts through a KVM
irqfd on the MSI route, published from the worker or completion thread.

Presents read a GPU-only frame from its image; dmabuf scanout for GL
displays (dmabuf, default on; 3 exportable images, driver-chosen DRM
modifier). Pipelines and samplers of deleted objects are destroyed with
the frame being recorded instead of after a queue idle. PVG_FRAMES=2..4.

defer-writeback (default off, experimental): render targets the guest
flags GPU-written stay on the GPU until SynchronizeResources, a present
or a reader of their pages; still loses some cross-process content.

Diagnostics: pvg_linux_exec_latency / _waits, pvg_linux_present_shown,
stale-read detector, PVG_DIAG_REDUNDANT.

Mission Control test: 17.0-17.6 -> 26.4-28.5 presents/s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
